  Full-duplex speech models (bolo.duplex)
    bolo.duplex.kyutai speaks Kyutai's WebSocket protocol for Moshi
    (message kinds and framing as documented in rust/protocol.md of
    https://github.com/kyutai-labs/moshi), and bolo.duplex.runtime carries the
    Moshi 7B language-model configuration values of moshi.models.loaders
    (moshi 0.2.13). Both are adapted from Kyutai's moshi, Copyright (c)
    Kyutai, licensed MIT (Python) and Apache-2.0 (Rust). The servers bolo
    installs on request keep their own licences and are not distributed with
    bolo: Kyutai's moshi and moshi_mlx (MIT), moshi-backend (MIT or
    Apache-2.0), hibiki-zero (MIT), and NVIDIA's PersonaPlex server (MIT; a
    fork of Kyutai's moshi). The tests use Kyutai's sphn (MIT or Apache-2.0).
    Model weights are downloaded by the user: Moshi and Hibiki CC-BY-4.0
    (credit Kyutai), Hibiki-Zero CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0, PersonaPlex the NVIDIA Open
    Model License, Kyutai's PersonaPlex fine-tune CC-BY-NC-4.0 with the NVIDIA
    Open Model License.
    CofeAI's FLM-Audio server (Apache-2.0) and Tencent's Freeze-Omni server
    (the Freeze-Omni License: academic, research and education use only) are
    installed on request the same way; bolo.duplex.socketio is bolo's own
    client of the Socket.IO protocol that Freeze-Omni's server speaks. Model
    weights: FLM-Audio states no licence (research use only per its model
    card), Freeze-Omni under its licence, with Qwen2-7B-Instruct (Apache-2.0).
    For MiniCPM-o 4.5 (Apache-2.0) bolo installs OpenBMB's minicpmo-utils
    (Apache-2.0) with PyTorch and runs its own server around the model's code.

  Speaker diarization uses the pyannote segmentation-3.0 model (MIT) in its
  ONNX export and a 3D-Speaker ERes2Net speaker-embedding model (Apache-2.0),
  both run by sherpa-onnx. Model files are downloaded by the user, not
  distributed with bolo. Optional speaker embedders, downloaded the same way:
  3D-Speaker ERes2Net and CAM++ (Apache-2.0), WeSpeaker CAM++ and ResNet34
  (CC-BY-4.0), NVIDIA TitaNet-small (CC-BY-4.0), all in sherpa-onnx's
  exports, and SpeechBrain's ECAPA-TDNN (Apache-2.0) in a third-party ONNX
  export run by ONNX Runtime.

  The optional diarization model "nemotron-3-diarization" is NVIDIA
  Nemotron-3-Diarization (https://huggingface.co/nvidia/Nemotron-3-Diarization),
  licensed under the OpenMDW License Agreement, version 1.1
  (https://openmdw.ai/license/1-1/), in the int8 ONNX conversion published
  by the onnx-community organisation on Hugging Face
  (https://huggingface.co/onnx-community/Nemotron-3-Diarization-ONNX). It is
  downloaded by the user on request, run by ONNX Runtime (MIT), and not
  distributed with bolo. Whoever redistributes the model files (for example
  inside a container image) must, under OpenMDW-1.1, include a copy of that
  agreement and keep the copyright and origin notices that come with the
  model. OpenMDW-1.1 also ends the licence of anyone who sues claiming that
  the model infringes a patent or copyright. bolo's NumPy implementation of
  the model's chunked inference (server/src/bolo/engines/nemotron.py: the
  speaker cache and chunk loop) follows the reference implementation in
  Hugging Face transformers (models/nemotron3_diarization, Apache-2.0,
  Copyright 2026 The HuggingFace Inc. team); its log-mel front-end
  (server/src/bolo/audio/features.py) is bolo's own implementation of NeMo's
  published preprocessing.

  Optional voice-activity detectors, downloaded by the user on request and
  not distributed with bolo: Silero VAD (MIT); TEN VAD (Agora; Apache-2.0
  with additional conditions: deployment only for the deployer's own
  applications and their direct end users, not in a way that competes with
  Agora's offerings or lets third parties build or deploy applications; see
  its LICENSE file, downloaded with the model); NVIDIA Frame-VAD Multilingual
  MarbleNet v2.0 (NVIDIA Open Model License) in a third-party ONNX export,
  whose log-mel front-end is computed by bolo's own code.

  eSpeak NG (GPL-3.0-or-later, https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng) is an
  OPTIONAL component. It is NOT bundled with bolo, not linked into it, not a
  dependency of the "all" extra and not in the default container images. It
  is installed only when the operator asks for it (the console, "bolo
  components install espeak-ng", the "espeak" extra, which pulls the
  third-party espeakng-loader wheel, or the system package manager) and
  after accepting its licence. Piper-format voices, KittenTTS, Matcha-TTS
  (English) and the non-English Kokoro voices need it; the default voice
  (Kokoro, English) does not. See docs/models.md, "Licences and the
  espeak-ng question".

Speech models run by bolo's own code
    server/src/bolo/engines/pivo.py follows the reference inference code of
    PiVo-1.4M (pivo/text.py, runtime.py, phase.py and audio.py at model revision
    515d84fc3420da6ae138f1b81017fcbc24d622a8, https://huggingface.co/empero-ai/PiVo-1.4M),
    Copyright 2026 Empero Research, Apache License 2.0; the PiVo model itself,
    with its FARGAN-derived vocoder (Xiph/Opus, BSD-3-Clause) and CMUdict data,
    is downloaded by the user and not distributed with bolo.
    server/src/bolo/engines/kale.py is a Python port of the text front end of
    kale-tts-1 (frontend/words/kaleTts.js and python/kale_tts.py at model
    revision b6ce1887268c3667994fc3b0b2c82887c069915c,
    https://huggingface.co/KaleComputer/kale-tts-1-nano), Copyright Kale
    Computer, Apache License 2.0; the model, its word lists (derived from
    CMUdict) and guesser are downloaded by the user and not distributed with bolo.

API compatibility
    The routes under "speaches-compatible" mirror the HTTP interface of
    speaches (https://github.com/speaches-ai/speaches, MIT licence,
    Copyright (c) 2024 Fedir Zadniprovskyi) so that its clients keep
    working. The interface was re-implemented from its route definitions;
    no speaches source code is included.

Models
    Models are not distributed with bolo. They are downloaded on request
    from their publishers, under the licences recorded for each entry in
    the catalog (server/src/bolo/catalog/*.yaml).

Fonts
    The web console bundles Inter and JetBrains Mono, and Noto Sans
    (Devanagari, Bengali, Tamil, Telugu, Gujarati, Kannada, Malayalam,
    Gurmukhi, Oriya) and Noto Naskh Arabic for text in those scripts, all
    under the SIL Open Font License 1.1, from the @fontsource packages.
    The word "बोलो" in the logo is outlined from Hind SemiBold
    (Copyright (c) 2014 Indian Type Foundry, SIL Open Font License 1.1);
    the font itself is not distributed.

Voices converted by this project
    The archives published as assets of the "voices-v1" release of this
    repository are format conversions (PyTorch checkpoint to ONNX, made
    with tools/voices/export_syspin.py) of the VITS voices published by
    the SYSPIN project, SPIRE Lab, Indian Institute of Science (IISc),
    Bengaluru (https://huggingface.co/SYSPIN). The model weights are under
    the MIT licence; they were trained on the SYSPIN text-to-speech
    corpus, licensed CC-BY-4.0. Each archive contains LICENSE and
    ATTRIBUTION files naming the source repository and revision. The
    archives are not part of the bolo package.

    The same release (and, when published there, Hugging Face repositories
    under codebucketsolutions/) may also carry format conversions of Meta's
    MMS-TTS voices (https://huggingface.co/facebook/mms-tts, made with
    tools/voices/export_mms.py and MMS's VITS inference code, MIT, Copyright
    (c) 2021 Jaehyeon Kim). Those weights are licensed CC-BY-NC-4.0:
    NON-COMMERCIAL use only. Each archive contains the licence text and the
    attribution.

    codebucketsolutions/pocket-tts-multilingual-GGUF on Hugging Face is a format
    conversion (GGUF, made with llama.cpp's converter) of Thanushttk's
    "Multilingual Pocket TTS" (https://huggingface.co/Thanushttk/pocket-tts-multilingual),
    a fine-tune of Kyutai Pocket TTS with Kyutai's Mimi codec weights, all
    CC-BY-4.0. Its model card carries the attribution and the licence text.

    The optional end-of-turn model is Smart Turn v3 by the Pipecat project
    (https://huggingface.co/pipecat-ai/smart-turn-v3, BSD-2-Clause),
    downloaded on request like any other model. bolo's Whisper-style
    feature computation (server/src/bolo/audio/features.py) is its own
    implementation of the published algorithm. Other optional end-of-turn
    models: VideoSDK's Namo Turn Detector v1 (Apache-2.0), run with the
    tokenizers library (Apache-2.0); Smart Turn v2 (BSD-2-Clause), whose
    published weights bolo computes with its own NumPy implementation of
    wav2vec2 (server/src/bolo/engines/smart_turn_v2.py, written from the
    model's published architecture and Pipecat's model.py); and TEN Turn
    Detection (Agora; Apache-2.0 with Agora's additional conditions, see its
    LICENSE file) in a third-party Q4_K_M GGUF conversion run by llama.cpp.

Web console: speech synthesis in the browser
    The console (and the live demo) can run text-to-speech voices in the
    page with ONNX Runtime Web (MIT, https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime),
    loaded from jsDelivr at run time, not bundled.

    ui/src/vendor/uccharan is a copy of the WebAssembly build of uccharan
    0.1.0, a text-to-phoneme library (Apache-2.0, Copyright 2026 Abhinav
    Gautam and the uccharan contributors, https://github.com/cb-Sigma/uccharan),
    with its LICENSE, NOTICE and DATA_LICENSES.md. Its English data file
    (en-us.ucd) is derived from the CMU Pronouncing Dictionary, Copyright (C)
    1993-2015 Carnegie Mellon University, BSD-2-Clause-style licence; the full
    text is in ui/src/vendor/uccharan/NOTICE. uccharan contains no eSpeak NG
    code or data; no GPL component is used for speech synthesis in the browser.

    ui/src/vendor/kale is a copy of the text front end of kale-tts-1
    (frontend/words/kaleTts.js and enGb.js with their type declarations, and
    frontend/dizione: Kale's JavaScript port of dizione's text rules and homograph
    picker), Copyright Kale Computer, Apache License 2.0, unchanged from
    https://huggingface.co/KaleComputer/kale-tts-1-nano at revision
    b6ce1887268c3667994fc3b0b2c82887c069915c, with its LICENSE and
    THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md. Its word lists (derived from CMUdict) and guesser are
    fetched from that repository in the visitor's browser. Paradee-8M, Kitten TTS 2
    Pico, little-tts (weights CC-BY-NC-4.0) and kale-tts-1-nano themselves are
    downloaded from Hugging Face in the browser, not distributed with bolo.

    The live demo serves, next to the page (under voices/), copies of these
    voices with their weights stored at reduced precision (8-bit and 16-bit;
    not retrained), made by ui/scripts/voices/build.py:
      - SYSPIN VITS voices (Hindi, Maithili, Bhojpuri, Marathi, Kannada and
        Indian English) by the SYSPIN project, SPIRE Lab, Indian Institute of
        Science, Bengaluru: weights MIT, training data the SYSPIN corpus,
        CC-BY-4.0 (see "Voices converted by this project" above);
      - the Piper voice en_US "amy" (low) from rhasspy/piper-voices (MIT),
        trained on the Amy dataset of Mycroft's mimic3-voices.
    Each voice directory carries its licence and attribution files. The
    voices are not part of the bolo package or of the git repository.

    The live demo's "Clone a voice" page downloads its models from Hugging
    Face at pinned revisions in the visitor's browser; none is distributed
    with bolo. Among them, MOSS-TTS-Nano 100M and MOSS-Audio-Tokenizer-Nano
    by the OpenMOSS team and MOSI.AI (Apache-2.0,
    https://github.com/OpenMOSS/MOSS-TTS-Nano), in their official ONNX
    exports. ui/src/lib/browser-clone/moss.ts and moss-text.ts follow that
    project's Apache-2.0 reference code (ort_cpu_runtime.py,
    onnx_tts_runtime.py and tts_robust_normalizer_single_script.py at
    commit 8b7bcc93), and moss-text.test.ts uses test cases from the latter.

Built-in audio samples
    The files in server/src/bolo/samples/*.wav were synthesised with bolo
    using the models named in server/src/bolo/samples/index.json. The live
    demo bundles a copy of hi-short.wav.
